<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! lar: URIs ~ one namespace, two l-spaces

`ha.ka.ba` names the STABLE l-space: a canonical, permanent, shared address. `lararium.local.vm`
names the volatile one, and each vessel's daemon VM owns its own.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! The invariants

# ''A volatile VM tiddler stands as SCRATCH.'' Nothing there persists through the island adaptor and nothing
syncs through Automerge. A vessel's daemon owns that namespace alone.
# ''The predicates answer NAMING, and nothing else.'' A stable address names a canonical,
permanent, shared address. ''It says nothing about federation and nothing about persistence'' — reading
either out of it fuses axes that move independently.
# ''Everything persists EXCEPT pure volatile scratch.'' A meme persists to the local store whether its
name reads stable or unstable; only the reserved VM space stays unwritten.
# ''An unstable petname carries any three-term root but the reserved one.'' A session name, a
per-relationship name, a per-place name — LOCAL, and no less real for it.
# ''A bag takes the shape `bags/slug`, and nothing else does.'' The grammar mints first segments that name no bag —
`tags/`, `state/`, the plugin memes, the bare-relation forms — so a predicate that took any first
segment for a bag would enrol half the namespace.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-dispatch-marker>>

!! The dispatch marker ~ the absence carries the safety

A genuine DOM verb-summon writes `lares-dispatch`. ''The verb machinery's own invocation and outcome
builders NEVER set it.''

That negative reading does the work: a synthesized record cannot pass for a summons by accident,
because passing requires a positive mark that only the door writes. A gate keyed on the presence of
machinery-written fields would admit anything the machinery could produce.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

